  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1257 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nWhile warm and breezy southerly flow will continue on Friday, we'll \nbe mostly cloudy as the weather system approaches and that will hold \ntemperatures down more in the mid 70s. Right now models continue to \ntrend a more delayed arrival of precipitation and the chances look \nto be isolated to the western Panhandle Friday evening.  Even as we \nget to Saturday the upper low may stall out and track almost due \nnorth toward the Four Corners. This will just continue that south to \nnorth flow real close to the TX/NM state line for most of the day \nSaturday. This would be the area to watch Friday night into Saturday \nafternoon for possible flooding concerns.  By Saturday night the low \nejects more northeast and that will pull the swath of moisture more \ninto the central Panhandles, as a result highs look to still be \npretty mild, in the 70s, across the eastern Panhandles with cooler \ntemperatures in the 60s for the west closer to the showers.  \n\nSunday looks to be the best day for widespread rain chances.  The \nfar southeast still looks to be on the mild side temperature wise as \nit might still miss out on the rain. As we move into Monday the \nupper disturbance is progged to track just north of the Panhandles \nand that will push that precipitation swath further south and that \nshould cover any areas in the southeast that may have missed \nprecipitation in previous days.  Highs on Monday and Tuesday look to \nbe in the 60s in the post frontal environment, and the rain chances \nshould really start to fall off Monday night as that front may be \nmostly through the Panhandles.  \n\nJust looking at the DESI probs for 1\" over the weekend, by Monday \nmorning the central and western Panhandles sit above 50% chance, and \nby Tuesday morning the entire Panhandles sits in the 30 to 60% \nrange.  Adding to that, the probs for 2\" of rain over the weekend \nthe central Panhandles is around 30% with numbers as high as 70% \nsouthwest of the Dalhart area.  When we look at Sunday through \nTuesday we are in the 10 to 50% range for the entire Panhandles, \nwith the central Panhandles having the higher probabilities.  So the \nsetup, while could have failures the dry slot the Panhandles, does \nlook more and more probable for increased rainfall, and possibly \nsome flooding concerns. \n\nWeber",
